course: world
lesson: 67
title: Clouds, rain, snow and hail
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A cloud is made of billions of tiny water droplets or ice crystals, so small that the gentlest rising air keeps them floating; a cloud is not water vapour, which is invisible. It takes about a million cloud droplets joined together to make one raindrop heavy enough to fall.
explain: Clouds have names by their shape and height: cumulus are white puffy heaps of fair weather; stratus are flat grey sheets that may bring drizzle; cirrus are thin wisps of ice high up; a cumulonimbus is a towering storm cloud that brings heavy rain, thunder and sometimes hail. Fog is simply a cloud lying on the ground.
explain: In cold clouds, raindrops usually start as ice: crystals grow on specks of dust, collect into snowflakes, and melt into rain on the way down if the air near the ground is warm. Every snowflake has six sides or six arms, because of the way water particles fit together when they freeze.
explain: Hail is made only in tall thunderstorm clouds: strong winds inside carry a frozen drop up and down again and again, and each trip adds a new layer of ice, like the layers of an onion, until the hailstone is too heavy and falls, even in summer.
explain: On clear calm nights the ground cools, and water vapour from the air condenses on cold grass as dew; when the grass is below freezing, the vapour turns straight into ice crystals called frost.
fact: Clouds are made of tiny water droplets or ice crystals, not of water vapour.
fact: About a million cloud droplets make one raindrop.
fact: Cumulus clouds are white and puffy and usually mean fair weather.
fact: Stratus clouds are low flat grey sheets.
fact: Cirrus clouds are thin wispy clouds of ice high in the sky.
fact: Cumulonimbus clouds are tall storm clouds that bring heavy rain, thunder and hail.
fact: Fog is a cloud that forms at ground level.
fact: Snowflakes have six sides or six arms.
fact: Hail forms in thunderstorm clouds as ice layers are added while strong winds carry it up and down.
fact: Thunderstorm clouds reach so high that their tops are far below freezing, even in summer.
fact: Dew is water vapour that condenses on cold surfaces; frost forms when the surface is below freezing.
fact: Clouds act like a blanket at night, so the ground loses the most heat on clear nights.
fact: Rainfall is measured with a rain gauge in millimetres.
exercise: What are clouds made of? | Tiny water droplets or ice crystals | Invisible water vapour floating in the air | Smoke from fires | Cotton-like dust
exercise: Why don't tiny cloud droplets fall? | They are so small that rising air keeps them up | They are lighter than air, like the gas in balloons | The Sun's warm rays pull them upward all day long | Cold air freezes them in place so they cannot fall
exercise: How many cloud droplets make one raindrop? | About a million | About ten | About a hundred | Exactly one
exercise: Which cloud is white and puffy and usually means fair weather? | Cumulus | Cumulonimbus | Stratus | Fog
exercise: Which cloud brings thunder and hail? | Cumulonimbus | Cirrus | Cumulus | Stratus
exercise: What are cirrus clouds made of? | Ice crystals high in the sky | Thick drops of rain in the sky | Dust blown up from hot deserts | Smoke rising from tall chimneys
exercise: What is fog? | A cloud at ground level | Smoke from far away fires | Steam from the sea that never rises | Dust stirred by the wind
exercise: How many sides or arms does a snowflake have? | Six | Four | Five | Eight
exercise: Where does hail form? | In tall thunderstorm clouds | In flat grey stratus clouds | On cold grass at night | In thin cirrus clouds
exercise: Why does a hailstone have layers like an onion? | It is carried up and down and gains a layer of ice each time | It is made of many snowflakes pressed flat | It freezes from the inside out on the ground | Different raindrops of different colours stick to it
exercise: What is dew? | Water vapour that condenses on cold surfaces | Rain that fell during the night | Water that plants sweat out of their roots | Water that rises up out of the ground at night
exercise: When does frost form instead of dew? | When the surface is below freezing | When the night is cloudy, warm and very still | When it is windy and wet | When the Sun is shining
exercise: What instrument measures how much rain falls? | A rain gauge | A thermometer | A wind vane | A barometer
exercise: Why can rain start as snow high in a cloud? | The top of the cloud is below freezing | Rain is always frozen when it is high in the sky | Snow is lighter than rain, so it falls instead | The Sun freezes the top of the cloud solid
exercise: Which cloud is a low flat grey sheet that may bring drizzle? | Stratus | Cirrus | Cumulus | Cumulonimbus
test: On a hot summer afternoon, a huge cloud grows tall like a tower and dark below. What should you expect? | A thunderstorm with heavy rain and perhaps hail | A light drizzle that lasts all week without stopping | Snow falling all evening | A calm clear night with frost
test: You breathe on a cold window and it turns misty. What is like this in nature? | Dew forming on cold grass at night | Hail growing in a storm cloud | Wind blowing over the sea | Lava cooling into rock
test: Why can hail fall even in hot summer? | Thunderstorm clouds reach so high that their tops are far below freezing | Summer air near the ground is below freezing | The Sun makes ice in the clouds | Hail is made on the ground and blown upward
test: After a clear calm night, the grass is white with ice, but after a cloudy night it is only wet. Why? | Without clouds the ground lost more heat and fell below freezing | Clouds bring ice to the grass | Cloudy nights are always colder | The Moon freezes the grass on clear nights when there is no cloud to hide it
test: Which is true about a snowflake? | It is made of ice crystals with six sides or arms | It is a frozen raindrop that is perfectly round like a ball | It is a piece of cloud that fell to the ground in one piece | It always has four arms, just like a cross or a plus sign
test: A climber high on a mountain walks into mist that hides everything. Where is he? | Inside a cloud | Inside a cloud of smoke | Under a rainbow | Above the sky
test: Why does a cut-open hailstone look like an onion? | It went up and down in the storm many times, adding a layer each time | It was made by many hailstones packed together on the ground | It grew roots like a plant in the cloud | It was dyed by different colours of rain
test: A pupil puts a straight-sided jar outside and finds 12 millimetres of water in it after one day. What has he made? | A simple rain gauge | A simple thermometer | A simple wind vane | A simple barometer
test: Snow falls high up, but it reaches the ground as rain. What does this tell you? | The air near the ground was warmer than freezing | The snow was never made of ice | The cloud was too low to snow | The ground was much colder than the cloud above it
test: why's fog so thick in the mornings, what even is it | A cloud that has formed at ground level | Smoke that has blown in from far away | Steam that rises up from the warm ground | Dust that is lifted by the morning wind
test: big tall dark clouds coming, what's that gonna be | A cumulonimbus storm cloud with heavy rain, maybe thunder and hail | A cirrus cloud bringing fine weather | A stratus cloud bringing a light breeze | A cumulus cloud bringing a clear sunny day
test: Leo says clouds are made of water vapour like steam. true? | No, clouds are tiny water droplets or ice crystals | Yes, clouds are pure water vapour floating high up in the sky | Yes, clouds are steam rising off the sea | No, clouds are made of smoke and dust
test: my brother said hail is just frozen rain falling once. right? | No, hail gets ice layers as winds carry it up and down in a storm cloud | Yes, hail is rain that froze on the way down | Yes, hail is snow that was squeezed into balls | No, hail is ice that falls from high cirrus clouds
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the composition of clouds? | Tiny water droplets or ice crystals | Invisible water vapour and nothing else | Smoke rising from fires and big factories | Dust raised high into the sky by the wind
exam: Identify the type of cloud which brings heavy rain, thunder and hail. | Cumulonimbus | Cirrus | Stratus | Cumulus
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning fog. | It is a cloud formed at ground level | It is smoke trapped near the ground | It is rain falling very slowly through cold air | It is frost which has melted
exam: It is given that water vapour condenses upon a surface whose temperature is below freezing. Ascertain the designation of the deposit so formed. | Frost | Dew | Hail | Sleet
